activities.content:
Writing a class in Java is simple. It consists of two primary things:

1. **Properties (Fields / Variables):** What the object *knows* (data).
2. **Behaviors (Methods):** What the object *can do* (actions).

```text
Class: Student

Properties (Data):
- name
- age
- course

Behaviors (Actions):
- study()
- displayProfile()
```

Here is how that exact blueprint looks in Java code:

```java
class Student {
    // 1. Properties (Variables declared inside the class)
    String name;
    int age;
    String course;

    // 2. Behavior (Method declared inside the class)
    void displayProfile() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name + " | Course: " + course);
    }
}
```

### Breaking It Down:
- `class Student`: We tell Java: *"I am defining a new blueprint named Student."*
- `{ ... }`: Everything between the curly braces belongs to this blueprint.
- `String name; int age; String course;`: Every student built from this blueprint will have their own `name`, `age`, and `course`.
- `void displayProfile() { ... }`: Every student built from this blueprint will know how to display their own profile!

> **Notice:** We have NOT introduced constructors, `private`, or getters/setters yet. You do not need any of those to write and understand your first classes!

activities.codeSnippet:
// A clean blueprint for a Book
class Book {
    // Properties
    String title;
    String author;
    int pages;

    // Behavior
    void readSummary() {
        System.out.println("Reading '" + title + "' by " + author + " (" + pages + " pages)");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Note: Writing the Book class above just created the blueprint!
        System.out.println("Book blueprint defined successfully!");
    }
}

